<Base
  title="IDEAS.md for AI coding projects — sudonotes docs"
  description="Link a sudonotes idea to a software project, mirror it to IDEAS.md, keep it gitignored, and give local coding agents useful project context."
  type="article"
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: "Docs", href: "/docs" }, { label: "Project linking" }]}
>
  <article class="page prose project-guide">
    <p class="eyebrow">Project linking</p>
    <h1>Keep project ideas next to the code with IDEAS.md</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Link an idea to a software project and sudonotes keeps a plain Markdown mirror at the project
      root. Your idea stays searchable in the vault while local coding agents can read its context
      where they are already working.
    </p>

    <div class="note">
      <strong><code>IDEAS.md</code> is a sudonotes convention, not an industry standard.</strong>
      The filename is deliberately predictable so people and tools know where to look.
    </div>

    <h2>Link an idea</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Open an idea in the desktop app.</li>
      <li>In the <strong>Project</strong> panel, choose <strong>Choose project folder…</strong>.</li>
      <li>Select the root folder of the software project.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      sudonotes records the project path in the vault note and writes the current note to
      <code>IDEAS.md</code>. The vault copy is canonical: every later save refreshes the project
      mirror. A missing or unwritable project never prevents the vault note itself from saving.
    </p>

    <h2>What appears in the project</h2>
    <pre><code>weather-station/
├── .git/
├── .gitignore       # contains IDEAS.md
├── IDEAS.md         # mirror managed by sudonotes
├── README.md
└── src/</code></pre>

    <p>The idea body can be as simple as:</p>

    <pre><code>## Offline alert queue

Store sensor alerts while the network is unavailable, then send them in order.

- Keep the queue bounded.
- Preserve the original timestamps.
- Show a visible retry state in the dashboard.</code></pre>

    <p>
      sudonotes writes the complete Markdown note, including its small YAML frontmatter, rather
      than converting it to a private format. A local coding agent with access to the working
      folder can read it like any other project file.
    </p>

    <h2>Git and privacy</h2>
    <p>
      In a Git repository, sudonotes adds <code>IDEAS.md</code> to <code>.gitignore</code>. That keeps
      the file available locally without sending the note to GitHub or including it in commits.
      Remote tools cannot see a gitignored file unless you give them separate access to your local
      working folder.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can deliberately remove the ignore entry and commit the file, but inspect both its body
      and frontmatter first. The mirror may contain private ideas and a local project path. Linking,
      unlinking, and local coding-agent access do not send the file through the sudonotes AI
      service.
    </p>

    <h2>If IDEAS.md already exists</h2>
    <p>sudonotes stops before overwriting it and gives you two choices:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Use the existing file</strong> imports its body into the vault idea, then links it.</li>
      <li><strong>Replace it with this note</strong> overwrites the project file with the open idea.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Cancel leaves both files unchanged.</p>

    <h2>Unlinking</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Unlink</strong> stops future mirroring and leaves the current <code>IDEAS.md</code> in place.</li>
      <li><strong>Unlink &amp; delete</strong> also removes the project mirror.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The <code>.gitignore</code> entry is kept so a later file with the same name is not committed by
      surprise. Remove that line manually if you no longer want it.
    </p>

    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button primary" href="/download">Download sudonotes</a>
      <a class="button" href="https://github.com/mory-dev/sudonotes">View the source on GitHub</a>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

